19:18:27 note  Updated its checklist
19:18:35 said  I built your own version of the app, called Codeloft. It copies the original's layout:
19:18:35 said  - **Home page:** your projects on the left, and your chats grouped into Today, Yesterday and Older on the right.
19:18:35 said  - **Chat screen:** a chat panel next to a side panel, where you can browse your project's files and see which ones the assistant changed.
19:18:35 said  - **Message box:** you can switch between "Build" (makes changes) and "Plan" (only looks), pick a model, or type commands yourself in shell mode.
19:18:35 said  - **Permission card:** the assistant stops and asks before it edits a file or runs a command, with "Allow once", "Allow always" or "Deny".
19:18:35 said  - **Also included:** a search-everything window (Ctrl+K), light and dark themes, and the original's keyboard shortcuts.
19:18:35 said  The app builds, and the comparison check passed: it starts and draws a screen that matches the original's look. I also tested it against a pretend AI service: it read a file, asked before editing it and before running a command, then finished.
19:18:35 said  The original couldn't tell me which AI service you wanted, so I decided it connects to one using your own key. Any service that works like OpenAI's is fine, including free ones that run on your computer. Until you enter a service address and key in Settings, it shows a message telling you to do that.
19:18:35 said  What's different from the original:
19:18:35 said  - It doesn't use the original's built-in sign-in or its free models.
19:18:35 said  - "Undo" removes your last message and its answers, but doesn't undo file changes.
19:18:35 said  - There's no built-in terminal, browser panel, voice input or remote-machine support.
19:18:35 said  - A few behind-the-scenes numbers are my estimates and are marked that way in its notes. These are the default permissions, how long a command may run, and how much output the assistant reads. The original keeps them in its hidden helper program, where I couldn't read them.
19:18:35 said  - Chat titles come from the first line of your first message; the original asks the AI to write them.
19:18:36 said  A good next step would be to tell me which AI service you use (OpenAI, OpenRouter, or a local one like Ollama). I can make it the default and then test a real chat with it.
